{"birds": [{"name": "Carolina Chickadee", "sci": "Poecile carolinensis", "n": 3, "first": "12:27 PM", "last": "12:29 PM", "ts": 1795282045, "mins": [747, 748, 749], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Chickadees hide seeds in many separate spots and can remember where for weeks, then dig them back out when food gets scarce.", "vox": "sounds/voice/poecile-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113028", "snd": "sounds/poecile-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113078", "sndby": "J.R. Rigby, xeno-canto XC309925, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "chickadee", "cm": 12.5, "img": "cutouts/chickadee.png?v=1793046043", "w": 686, "h": 800, "len": 829.0, "v": 1793046043, "beak": [0.78, 0.05, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 37, "b": "AAB+AQAA/8MAAf+DAAP/gwAD/wYAA/8GAAf/PAAH/7wAB//8AA//+AAf//4AH/+/AD//vwA//38Af/98AP//5AD//sAA//3AAf/9gAH//4AD//+AA///gAf//4APg/wADgH8ABwA+AA4APgAeAH4AHAB8ADgA/AAwAeAAMAPAAAAHwAAADwAAAA8AAAAeAAAADAAAA=="}}, {"name": "American Goldfinch", "sci": "Spinus tristis", "n": 9, "first": "12:32 PM", "last": "3:03 PM", "ts": 1795282322, "mins": [752, 862, 863, 863, 864, 864, 865, 889, 903], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "One of the strictest vegetarians among birds. Goldfinches nest late in summer, when thistle seeds are ripe, and swap bright yellow for olive-brown in winter.", "vox": "sounds/voice/spinus-tristis.mp3?v=1793113053", "snd": "sounds/spinus-tristis.mp3?v=1793113090", "sndby": "Will Sweet, xeno-canto XC453934, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "goldfinch", "cm": 12.0, "img": "cutouts/goldfinch.png?v=1793990895", "w": 451, "h": 369, "len": 415.0, "v": 1793990895, "beak": [0.206, 0.15, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 26, "b": "ADwAAAD+AAAB/wAAA/8AAAP/gAAA/8AAAH/AAAD/8AAA//AAAP/4DwD/+B4Af/w4AH/+8AA//8AAP/8AAB//AAAH/4AAB//AAA/PwAAeQeAA/gHwAfgA+APIAHwPAAAgHAAAADgAAAA="}}, {"name": "Northern Cardinal", "sci": "Cardinalis cardinalis", "n": 1, "first": "12:32 PM", "last": "12:32 PM", "ts": 1795282357, "mins": [752], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Female cardinals sing too, which is unusual for a North American songbird. During courtship the male feeds her seeds beak to beak.", "vox": "sounds/voice/cardinalis-cardinalis.mp3?v=1793112978", "snd": "sounds/cardinalis-cardinalis.mp3?v=1793113085", "sndby": "David Tattersley, xeno-canto XC1000248, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "cardinal", "cm": 22.0, "img": "cutouts/cardinal.png?v=1793031428", "w": 516, "h": 348, "len": 530.0, "v": 1793031428, "beak": [0.06, 0.28, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 22, "b": "AYAAAAeAAAAPgAAAH4AAAB/AAAA/4AAAf/gAAB/+AAAf/4AAH//AAA//8AAP//gAB//8AAf//AAD//+AAf//wEB/v/jwf3v+///+/z//vn945gA+MAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Downy Woodpecker", "sci": "Dryobates pubescens", "n": 20, "first": "12:57 PM", "last": "4:09 PM", "ts": 1795283868, "mins": [777, 778, 833, 856, 887, 887, 888, 888, 905, 939, 939, 940, 940, 941, 942, 942, 952, 952, 957, 969], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "North America’s smallest woodpecker. It is small enough to forage on thin weed stalks and twigs that bigger woodpeckers can’t cling to.", "vox": "sounds/voice/dryobates-pubescens.mp3?v=1793112995", "snd": "sounds/dryobates-pubescens.mp3?v=1793113092", "sndby": "Thomas G. Graves, xeno-canto XC89855, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "downy", "cm": 16.0, "img": "cutouts/downy.png?v=1793045350", "w": 486, "h": 800, "len": 707.0, "v": 1793045350, "beak": [0.59, 0.1, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 53, "b": "AAAAAP/4AAD//AAA//wAAP/8IAD//DwA//wf4H/8D/x//Af+f/wH/3/8A/9//AP/f/wD/3/8B/9//A//f/wf/n/8P/x//H/8f/7//H/+//x////8f////D////x////8f////H////h////4f///+D////A////wP///4D///+A////gP///wD///8A///+AP///gD///wA///4AP//+AD///AA///wAP//sAD//4AA//8AAP//AAD//wAA//4AAP/+AAD//gAA//4AAP/8AAD//AAA="}}, {"name": "Eastern Bluebird", "sci": "Sialia sialis", "n": 2, "first": "12:58 PM", "last": "3:10 PM", "ts": 1795283927, "mins": [778, 910], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Bluebirds nest in tree cavities, and their numbers crashed in the early 1900s when starlings and house sparrows took the holes. Volunteer nest-box trails helped them rebound.", "vox": "sounds/voice/sialia-sialis.mp3?v=1793113046", "snd": "sounds/sialia-sialis.mp3?v=1793113130", "sndby": "Robin Carter, xeno-canto XC1374, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "bluebird", "cm": 18.0, "img": "cutouts/bluebird.png?v=1793990893", "w": 649, "h": 609, "len": 744.0, "v": 1793990893, "beak": [0.851, 0.1, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 30, "b": "AAA/AAAA/4AAAP/wAAH/4AAD/4AAA/+AAAP/gAAH/4AAD/+AAA//gAAf/4AAP/+AAD//gAB//4AA//8AAf/+AgH//g4D//w8A//48Af/98AP//8AH8b8AB8P8AAcPzAAOfgwAHPgAADvgAAA3gAAAHgAAABwAAAA"}}, {"name": "Cedar Waxwing", "sci": "Bombycilla cedrorum", "n": 1, "first": "1:17 PM", "last": "1:17 PM", "ts": 1795285039, "mins": [797], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "The red tips on its wing feathers look like drops of sealing wax, which gave waxwings their name. They sometimes pass berries beak to beak along a row of perched birds.", "vox": "sounds/voice/bombycilla-cedrorum.mp3?v=1793112972", "snd": "sounds/bombycilla-cedrorum.mp3?v=1793113168", "sndby": "Rory Nefdt, xeno-canto XC1142128, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "waxwing", "cm": 17.0, "img": "cutouts/waxwing.png?v=1793045350", "w": 800, "h": 738, "len": 708.0, "v": 1793045350, "beak": [0.61, 0.21, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 30, "b": "AAAADgAAAAwAAAAMAA8ADwAfwB8AD/AfAB/x2AAf4fgAP8H4AH+B/AD/xPwA/8X4Af/HgAP/hwAD/4YAB/+OAA//nAAP/zAAD/5wAB/9wAA//4AAP/8AAH/eAAA8+gAAeHAAAHDgAADj4AAAw4AAAEcAAAAGAAAA"}}, {"name": "Fish Crow", "sci": "Corvus ossifragus", "n": 4, "first": "1:54 PM", "last": "2:58 PM", "ts": 1795287277, "mins": [834, 835, 835, 898], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "It looks just like an American Crow. The giveaway is the voice: a nasal, two-note “uh-uh” instead of a clear “caw.”", "vox": "sounds/voice/corvus-ossifragus.mp3?v=1793112991", "snd": "sounds/corvus-ossifragus.mp3?v=1793113105", "sndby": "Mike Nelson, xeno-canto XC130581, CC BY-NC-SA 3.0", "key": "fishcrow", "cm": 38.0, "img": "cutouts/fishcrow.png?v=1793990894", "w": 484, "h": 472, "len": 557.0, "v": 1793990894, "beak": [0.021, 0.08, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 31, "b": "D4AAAH/AAAD/4AAAf/AAAA/4AAAH/AAAB/4AAAf/gAAH/8AAB//gAAf/+AAH//wAA//+AAH//wAB//+AAP//gAA//8AAH//gAA//+AAHZ/wAB2P8AA5h+AAcwP4f/uA+P//+Dj////4////+P////gD///4AAD/+AAAAPg=="}}, {"name": "House Finch", "sci": "Haemorhous mexicanus", "n": 1, "first": "2:17 PM", "last": "2:17 PM", "ts": 1795288662, "mins": [857], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Native to the West, house finches were released on Long Island in 1940 as pet-shop “Hollywood finches” and spread across the East. Redder males are favored by females.", "vox": "sounds/voice/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113005", "snd": "sounds/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113108", "sndby": "Ted Floyd, xeno-canto XC622745, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "housefinch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/housefinch.png?v=1793046043", "w": 800, "h": 581, "len": 662.0, "v": 1793046043, "beak": [0.12, 0.13, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "B+AAAAfwAQoP8AHOH/gB7wf8Af8D/gH/B/8A3wf/AP4H/4zgB//fwAP/zwAD//4AAf/+AAD//AAAf/gAAD/8AA/+/AAH4B4AB+APAB6AD4A4AAeA8AAAAMAAAAA="}}, {"name": "White-breasted Nuthatch", "sci": "Sitta carolinensis", "n": 8, "first": "2:36 PM", "last": "4:15 PM", "ts": 1795289797, "mins": [876, 877, 905, 905, 906, 906, 974, 975], "anim": null, "trunk": true, "fact": "Nuthatches climb down tree trunks head first. The name comes from “nut-hack”: they wedge a seed into bark and hack it open.", "vox": "sounds/voice/sitta-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113050", "snd": "sounds/sitta-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113095", "sndby": "Andrew Spencer, xeno-canto XC14860, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "nuthatch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/nuthatch.png?v=1793031430", "w": 532, "h": 327, "len": 372.0, "v": 1793031430, "beak": [0.79, 0.42, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 20, "b": "fgAAAH8AAAB/AAAAP4AAAD+AAAAfwAAAH/4gAB///gAf///AB///gAf//gAD//wAA//4AAP/8AAB//AAAf/AAAD+AAAA/gAAAP4AAAB/AAA="}}, {"name": "Dark-eyed Junco", "sci": "Junco hyemalis", "n": 14, "first": "2:54 PM", "last": "4:13 PM", "ts": 1795290871, "mins": [894, 895, 895, 908, 910, 911, 912, 912, 913, 913, 913, 953, 973, 973], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Often called “snowbirds” because they show up as winter arrives. The white outer tail feathers flash as they flit away.", "vox": "sounds/voice/junco-hyemalis.mp3?v=1793113009", "snd": "sounds/junco-hyemalis.mp3?v=1793113154", "sndby": "Phoenix Birder, xeno-canto XC563632, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "junco", "cm": 15.5, "img": "cutouts/junco.png?v=1793991313", "w": 534, "h": 447, "len": 625.0, "v": 1793991313, "beak": [0.022, 0.14, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 27, "b": "D4AAAD/AAAA/4AAA//AAAP/wAAA//AAAH/4AAD//AAA//4AAP//AAD//4AA///AAH//4wB///cAP//+AB///AAf//wAB//+AAP//wAA//8AAH+PgAD8B8AH+AHgD/gA8D+AAHg/gAA8AAAAH"}}, {"name": "Red-tailed Hawk", "sci": "Buteo jamaicensis", "n": 1, "first": "3:38 PM", "last": "3:38 PM", "ts": 1795293495, "mins": [938], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "That fierce scream used in movies for eagles is really a Red-tailed Hawk. A real Bald Eagle sounds much weaker and chirpier.", "vox": "sounds/voice/buteo-jamaicensis.mp3?v=1793112976", "snd": "sounds/buteo-jamaicensis.mp3?v=1793113137", "sndby": "Allen T. Chartier, xeno-canto XC31162, CC BY-NC-SA 3.0", "key": "redtailed", "cm": 51.0, "img": "cutouts/redtailed.png?v=1793990896", "w": 740, "h": 589, "len": 522.0, "v": 1793990896, "beak": [0.389, 0.12, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 25, "b": "A+AAAAfwAAAP+AAAD/gAAA//AAAP/8AAD//wAB//+AAf//wAD//+Bwf//i4D//+4Af//8AD//+AAP//wAD//8AAf+fgAH+B4AD/gAAB/AAAA/gAAAfwAAA/gAAA/AAAA/AAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Carolina Wren", "sci": "Thryothorus ludovicianus", "n": 1, "first": "4:10 PM", "last": "4:10 PM", "ts": 1795295408, "mins": [970], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its song sounds like “teakettle, teakettle, teakettle,” and pairs often sing duets. It will nest in odd places: flowerpots, mailboxes, even old boots.", "vox": "sounds/voice/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113060", "snd": "sounds/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113075", "sndby": "Jacob Saucier, xeno-canto XC498646, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "wren", "cm": 13.0, "img": "cutouts/wren.png?v=1793046044", "w": 800, "h": 633, "len": 781.0, "v": 1793046044, "beak": [0.85, 0.21, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 25, "b": "OAAAADgAAAA8AAAAHgB+AB4B/+APA//wB5//AAf//gAH//4AB//+AAf//AAH//wAD//4AAP/+AAB//H/AP/f/wA///8AGf/cAB/7AA//8AAP/4AAP/8AAP9gAAD8AAAAcAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Rock Pigeon", "sci": "Columba livia", "n": 1, "first": "4:30 PM", "last": "4:30 PM", "ts": 1795296632, "mins": [990], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Pigeons have been domesticated for thousands of years, and homing pigeons can find their way back across hundreds of miles.", "vox": "sounds/voice/columba-livia.mp3?v=1793986166", "snd": "sounds/columba-livia.mp3?v=1793113284", "sndby": "Paul Marvin, xeno-canto XC485507, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "rockpigeon", "cm": 32.0, "img": "cutouts/rockpigeon.png?v=1793987498", "w": 800, "h": 544, "len": 872.0, "v": 1793987498, "beak": [0.994, 0.201, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 22, "b": "AAAAPgAAAH4AAAB+AAAA/wAAB/0AAD/8AAD//AAB//wAB//8AA///AAf//wAP//8A///+AP///gB///wA///wA/h/wA+AB4A+AD/4OAA/+AAAP/gAAD/4A=="}}, {"name": "Tufted Titmouse", "sci": "Baeolophus bicolor", "n": 1, "first": "4:42 PM", "last": "4:42 PM", "ts": 1795297328, "mins": [1002], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Titmice line their nests with hair, and they have been seen plucking it from live raccoons, dogs, and even people.", "vox": "sounds/voice/baeolophus-bicolor.mp3?v=1793112971", "snd": "sounds/baeolophus-bicolor.mp3?v=1793113083", "sndby": "Jim Berry, xeno-canto XC649331, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "titmouse", "cm": 16.5, "img": "cutouts/titmouse.png?v=1793046043", "w": 574, "h": 800, "len": 653.0, "v": 1793046043, "beak": [0.2, 0.25, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 45, "b": "cAAAAHAAAAAwAAAAIAAAAGgDAAB8B4AAPB8AAD5/AAA+/4AAeP+AAHv/wAB7/8AA+P/gAPhn4ACbH/AADz/wAA9/+AAPf/wABb/8AB+//gAO//4ABt//AAfP/wAHx/+AB+P/gAPh/8AHiP/AB47/wAeH/+AHofvgBvOB8AD3APgAfAB4APgAPAD4A